/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/aws-cloud-practitioner.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("aws-cloud-practitioner", [["clf1",0,1,"A startup cannot predict how many users its new app will have at launch and does not want to buy servers that may sit idle. Which cloud benefit addresses this most directly?",["Trade variable expense for capital expense","Stop guessing capacity by scaling with demand","Gain exclusive use of dedicated hardware","Hand off all responsibility for the application"],1,"In the cloud you provision what you need now and scale up or down as demand changes, so you no longer guess capacity. The first option reverses the real benefit: the cloud trades capital expense for variable expense.","Task 1.1",["This reverses the actual advantage: the cloud trades capital (fixed) expense for variable expense, not the other way round.",null,"Dedicated hardware (Dedicated Hosts or Instances) addresses licensing or isolation needs, not uncertainty about how many users will arrive.","The customer never hands off all responsibility; security in the cloud, data and the application itself remain the customer's job."],null],["clf2",0,1,"What does 'trade fixed expense for variable expense' mean for an organization moving to AWS?",["It pays up front for servers that depreciate over several years","It signs a fixed multi-year contract that covers every service","It pays only for the IT resources it uses, as it uses them","It buys hardware in bulk to lower the price of each server"],2,"Instead of investing heavily in data centers and servers before knowing how they will be used, you pay only when you consume resources. Up-front purchases and bulk buying are the fixed-expense model the cloud replaces.","Task 1.1",["Paying up front for depreciating servers is capital expense, which is exactly what the cloud lets you move away from.","A fixed multi-year contract for every service is a fixed commitment, the opposite of paying variably for usage.",null,"Bulk hardware purchasing is an on-premises capital purchase; it describes economies of scale for the buyer, not variable expense."],null],["clf3",0,1,"A company based in Canada wants to launch its web app for customers in Japan and Germany next week, with low latency for both. Which cloud advantage makes this practical?",["Go global in minutes","Economies of scale","Stateless design","Consolidated billing"],0,"AWS Regions around the world let a company deploy close to users in a few clicks, without building data centers. Economies of scale lower prices but do not place workloads near users.","Task 1.1",[null,"Economies of scale explain why AWS prices are low because of aggregated usage; they do not put your app near users in Japan and Germany.","Stateless design is an application architecture pattern that helps scaling, but it is not the advantage that lets you deploy to new countries quickly.","Consolidated billing combines charges from multiple accounts in AWS Organizations and has nothing to do with deploying near users."],null],["clf4",0,1,"An online store's traffic triples during a holiday sale and drops back afterward. The company wants capacity to be added and removed automatically to match. Which concept describes this?",["Durability","Fault tolerance","Agility","Elasticity"],3,"Elasticity is the ability to acquire resources as you need them and release them when you no longer do, automatically. Fault tolerance is about continuing to operate when components fail, not about matching load.","Task 1.1",["Durability is about not losing stored data, such as S3's design for eleven nines; it does not add or remove capacity.","Fault tolerance keeps a system running through component failures; it is not about matching capacity to changing demand.","Agility is the speed of provisioning and experimenting; automatic scaling in and out with demand is elasticity.",null],null],["clf5",0,1,"Which AWS Well-Architected pillar focuses on a workload recovering from failures and performing its intended function consistently?",["Reliability","Performance efficiency","Operational excellence","Sustainability"],0,"The reliability pillar covers recovering from infrastructure or service disruptions and meeting demand. Performance efficiency is about using resources efficiently as demand and technology change.","Task 1.2",[null,"Performance efficiency is about using the right resources efficiently for performance, not about recovering from failures.","Operational excellence covers running and improving operations, such as operations as code, rather than consistent recovery from failure.","Sustainability focuses on reducing environmental impact, not on the workload recovering from failures."],null],["clf6",0,1,"A company's board asks IT to reduce the energy and environmental impact of its cloud workloads, for example by maximizing utilization and choosing efficient instance types. Which Well-Architected pillar covers this goal?",["Sustainability","Reliability","Cost optimization","Security"],0,"Sustainability, the sixth pillar, focuses on minimizing the environmental impact of workloads. Cost optimization often aligns with it but is about avoiding unnecessary spending, not environmental impact.","Task 1.2",[null,"Reliability is about recovering from failures and performing consistently, not about energy or environmental impact.","Cost optimization is about spending less; maximizing utilization can save money, but the stated goal of reducing environmental impact is the sustainability pillar.","Security protects data and systems; it does not address energy use or environmental impact."],null],["clf7",0,1,"An architect places an Amazon SQS queue between a web tier and an order-processing tier so that a slow or failed processor does not break the website. Which design principle is being applied?",["Vertical scaling","Tight coupling","Manual provisioning","Loose coupling"],3,"Loose coupling reduces dependencies so one component can fail or slow down without taking others with it; a queue is the classic tool. Tight coupling is the opposite and makes failures spread.","Task 1.2",["Vertical scaling means making a single resource larger; adding a queue between tiers does not change any instance's size.","Tight coupling is what the queue removes; tightly coupled tiers fail together when one slows down.","Manual provisioning is about how resources are created, not how tiers communicate; a queue is an architectural decoupling choice.",null],null],["clf8",0,1,"Which of the following is a design principle of the Well-Architected operational excellence pillar?",["Deploy everything into a single Availability Zone","Perform operations as code","Buy capacity for peak demand up front","Encrypt only the data that is in transit"],1,"Operational excellence recommends defining operations (infrastructure, procedures, responses) as code to reduce human error. Buying for peak demand is the capacity guessing the cloud is meant to avoid.","Task 1.2",["A single Availability Zone creates a single point of failure and contradicts Well-Architected guidance; it is not an operational excellence principle.",null,"Buying for peak up front is the 'guessing capacity' approach the framework tells you to stop, not an operational excellence principle.","The security pillar recommends protecting data both in transit and at rest; encrypting only transit data is not a design principle."],null],["clf9",0,1,"A solutions team wants a free console tool where it can document a workload, answer questions for each Well-Architected pillar and get an improvement plan. Which service should it use?",["AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS Config","AWS Artifact","AWS Well-Architected Tool"],3,"The AWS Well-Architected Tool walks you through the pillar questions for a defined workload and tracks improvements. Trusted Advisor checks your deployed resources automatically but does not run a question-based review.","Task 1.2",["Trusted Advisor automatically checks resources in your account for best practices; it does not walk you through pillar questions about a workload.","AWS Config records resource configurations and evaluates them against rules; it does not run Well-Architected reviews.","AWS Artifact provides AWS compliance reports and agreements, not workload reviews or improvement plans.",null],null],["clf10",0,1,"Which list names the six perspectives of the AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF)?",["Compute, storage, network, database, security, billing","Plan, build, test, release, operate, monitor","Business, people, governance, platform, security, operations","Identify, protect, detect, respond, recover, audit"],2,"AWS CAF groups capabilities into business, people, governance, platform, security and operations perspectives. The last option resembles a cybersecurity framework's functions, not AWS CAF.","Task 1.3",["These are categories of AWS services, not the CAF's perspectives on organizational capabilities.","This resembles software delivery life cycle stages, not the CAF perspectives.",null,"These resemble cybersecurity framework functions (such as NIST CSF), not the AWS CAF perspectives."],null],["clf11",0,1,"During its cloud migration, a company worries that staff lack cloud skills and that teams will resist new ways of working. Which AWS CAF perspective addresses this?",["Platform","Governance","People","Operations"],2,"The people perspective covers culture, organizational change, training and workforce transformation. Governance deals with program management, risk and cost control rather than skills.","Task 1.3",["The Platform perspective covers building the cloud platform and architectures, not staff skills and cultural change.","The Governance perspective covers program management, risk and cloud financial management rather than training and resistance to change.",null,"The Operations perspective covers running services day to day, such as monitoring and incident management, not workforce skills."],null],["clf12",0,1,"A company must migrate an on-premises Oracle database to Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL while keeping the source database available during the move. Which combination fits?",["AWS DataSync and Amazon S3","AWS Application Migration Service alone","AWS DMS with the AWS Schema Conversion Tool","AWS Backup and a restore into Aurora"],2,"AWS DMS migrates data (with ongoing replication so the source stays in use), and the Schema Conversion Tool converts the schema between different engines. Application Migration Service rehosts whole servers; it does not convert Oracle to PostgreSQL.","Task 1.3",["DataSync moves files and objects, not a live database with schema conversion, and S3 is not the target database here.","Application Migration Service rehosts whole servers to EC2; it cannot convert an Oracle database into Aurora PostgreSQL.",null,"AWS Backup protects AWS resources; it cannot back up an on-premises Oracle database and restore it into a different engine while the source stays online."],null],["clf13",0,1,"A company wants to lift and shift 40 on-premises virtual machines to Amazon EC2 with continuous block-level replication and a short cutover window. Which service is designed for this?",["AWS Database Migration Service","AWS Elastic Beanstalk","AWS Storage Gateway","AWS Application Migration Service"],3,"AWS Application Migration Service replicates source servers into AWS and launches them as EC2 instances at cutover, which is the rehost strategy. DMS moves databases, not whole servers.","Task 1.3",["AWS DMS migrates databases, not entire virtual machines with block-level replication.","Elastic Beanstalk deploys application code onto managed environments; it does not replicate existing VMs to EC2.","Storage Gateway gives on-premises applications access to cloud storage; it is not a server migration tool.",null],null],["clf14",0,1,"In the 7 Rs of migration, what does 'rehost' mean?",["Moving an application to the cloud without changing it","Rewriting the application as serverless microservices","Replacing the application with a SaaS product","Shutting down an application nobody uses anymore"],0,"Rehost, or lift and shift, moves the application as it is. Rewriting it is refactor, replacing it with SaaS is repurchase and shutting it down is retire.","Task 1.3",[null,"Rewriting as serverless microservices is refactor (re-architect), which involves significant change.","Replacing an application with a SaaS product is repurchase.","Shutting down an unused application is retire."],null],["clf15",0,1,"A company decides to stop running its self-hosted CRM software and move to a subscription-based SaaS CRM instead. Which migration strategy is this?",["Rehost","Repurchase","Replatform","Retain"],1,"Repurchase means moving to a different product, usually SaaS. Replatform keeps the same application but makes some cloud optimizations.","Task 1.3",["Rehost moves the same software to AWS unchanged; here the company stops using its CRM software entirely.",null,"Replatform keeps the same application with small cloud optimizations; switching to a different SaaS product is repurchase.","Retain means keeping the application where it is for now, but the company is replacing it."],null],["clf16",0,1,"A team moves its MySQL database from a self-managed server to Amazon RDS for MySQL without changing application code, so it no longer patches or backs up the database itself. Which strategy is this?",["Replatform","Refactor","Retire","Relocate"],0,"Replatform (lift, tinker and shift) makes targeted optimizations, such as a managed database, without re-architecting. Refactor would change the application's architecture and code.","Task 1.3",[null,"Refactor means re-architecting the application with cloud-native services; here the code is unchanged.","Retire means decommissioning the application, but this database keeps running.","Relocate moves infrastructure such as a VMware environment without changes; moving to a managed service like RDS is replatform."],null],["clf17",0,1,"A media company needs to copy 150 TB of files from an on-premises NAS to Amazon S3 over its network, on a schedule, with data integrity checks. Which service should it use?",["AWS Backup","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","AWS Direct Connect gateway","AWS DataSync"],3,"DataSync automates and accelerates online transfers between on-premises storage and AWS storage services, with scheduling and verification. Kinesis Data Streams handles real-time streaming records, not bulk file copies.","Task 1.3",["AWS Backup centrally manages backups of AWS resources; it is not designed for scheduled bulk transfer from an on-premises NAS.","Kinesis Data Streams ingests real-time streaming records, not bulk file copies from a NAS.","A Direct Connect gateway is a networking construct for dedicated connections; it provides a network path but does not copy or verify files.",null],null],["clf18",0,1,"How does AWS's economies of scale benefit customers?",["Customers must commit to larger purchases to get any discount","Every customer receives its own dedicated data center","AWS's huge combined usage lets it offer lower prices","Prices are fixed for the whole life of each account"],2,"Because AWS aggregates usage from many customers, it achieves economies of scale and passes them on as lower pay-as-you-go prices. No commitment is required for this benefit.","Task 1.4",["Economies of scale benefit all customers through lower pay-as-you-go prices; they do not require larger purchases to get any discount.","Customers share AWS's infrastructure; nobody receives a dedicated data center, and that would work against economies of scale.",null,"AWS prices are not fixed for an account's life; economies of scale have historically led AWS to lower prices over time."],null],["clf19",0,1,"A finance team compares running 20 servers in its own data center with running them on Amazon EC2. Which cost is typically eliminated by moving to EC2?",["Buying and refreshing the physical servers","Licensing for all application software","Staff time to design the application","Charges for data sent out to the internet"],0,"AWS owns and refreshes the hardware, so the company no longer buys servers. Software licensing and application design still exist, and outbound data transfer is actually a new AWS charge.","Task 1.4",[null,"Application software licensing continues in the cloud, either bought separately (BYOL) or included in the instance price.","Staff still design, build and secure the application; moving to EC2 does not remove that work.","Data transfer out to the internet is still charged on AWS, so this cost is not eliminated."],null],["clf20",0,1,"A company wants to reuse its existing Windows Server licenses, which are tied to physical cores, when it moves to AWS. Which EC2 option supports this bring-your-own-license need?",["Spot Instances","A Compute Savings Plan","Dedicated Hosts","Instances with license included"],2,"Dedicated Hosts give you a physical server with visibility into its sockets and cores, which many per-core or per-socket licenses require. License-included instances bundle the license cost, which is the opposite of bringing your own.","Task 1.4",["Spot Instances run on shared spare capacity and can be interrupted; they give no visibility into physical cores for licensing.","A Savings Plan is a pricing commitment; it does not provide dedicated physical hardware or core visibility.",null,"License-included instances bundle a new license in the price, so the company would not be reusing its existing licenses."],null],["clf21",0,1,"An EC2 instance has averaged 4% CPU and 10% memory use for three months. Which cloud economics practice should the company apply first?",["Buy a Reserved Instance for its current size","Rightsize it to a smaller instance type","Move it to a Dedicated Host","Add a second instance for redundancy"],1,"Rightsizing matches the instance type to real usage, cutting cost immediately. Committing to a Reserved Instance for an oversized instance would lock in the waste.","Task 1.4",["Reserving the current size would lock in a commitment for an instance that is far too large, making the waste harder to fix.",null,"A Dedicated Host is for licensing or isolation needs and would add cost; it does nothing about low utilization.","Adding a second instance improves availability but doubles the cost of capacity that is already mostly idle."],null],["clf22",0,1,"A development team can now try a new idea in the morning, test it on AWS by afternoon and delete the resources if it fails, at little cost. Which cloud benefit does this show?",["Durability","Compliance","Tenancy","Agility"],3,"Agility means resources are available in minutes, so the cost and time of experimenting drop sharply. Durability refers to data not being lost, not to speed of experimentation.","Task 1.1",["Durability refers to protecting stored data from loss, not to how quickly teams can experiment.","Compliance is about meeting regulations and standards, not about speed of trying new ideas.","Tenancy describes whether hardware is shared or dedicated, which has nothing to do with experimentation speed.",null],null],["clf23",0,2,"A company runs an application on Amazon EC2 instances. Under the shared responsibility model, who is responsible for applying security patches to the guest operating system?",["AWS, as part of managing the hypervisor","The customer who runs the instances","The seller of the AMI in AWS Marketplace","AWS Support, once a case is opened"],1,"With EC2 (infrastructure as a service), the customer manages the guest OS, including patching. AWS is responsible for the host, hypervisor and physical infrastructure beneath it.","Task 2.1",["AWS manages the hypervisor and host, but the guest operating system inside an EC2 instance is under the customer's control.",null,"A Marketplace seller supplies the AMI, but once you launch and run the instance, patching its OS is your responsibility.","AWS Support gives guidance, but opening a case does not transfer responsibility for patching your instances to AWS."],null],["clf24",0,2,"Which task is AWS's responsibility as part of 'security of the cloud'?",["Configuring security group rules for EC2","Choosing encryption settings for S3 objects","Rotating passwords for IAM users","Physical security of the data centers"],3,"AWS protects the facilities, hardware and global infrastructure. Security groups, encryption settings and IAM passwords are configuration choices the customer makes (security in the cloud).","Task 2.1",["Security group rules are configured by the customer; that is security in the cloud.","Choosing encryption settings for your S3 objects is a customer decision about protecting their own data.","Managing and rotating IAM user passwords is part of the customer's identity and access responsibility.",null],null],["clf25",0,2,"A company moves its database to Amazon RDS. Which task remains the customer's responsibility?",["Replacing failed storage hardware","Patching the host operating system","Managing database users and their permissions","Installing the database engine software"],2,"With RDS, AWS manages the host OS, hardware and engine installation, but the customer still controls who can access the database and with what privileges. Hardware and host OS belong to AWS.","Task 2.1",["With RDS, AWS manages the physical storage hardware and replaces failed components.","RDS is a managed service, so AWS patches the host operating system.",null,"AWS installs and maintains the database engine software on RDS instances."],null],["clf26",0,2,"A developer builds an application on AWS Lambda. Which item is the developer responsible for?",["The function code and the permissions it is given","Scaling the servers that run the function","Patching the OS beneath the runtime","The physical network in the Region"],0,"With serverless services AWS manages servers, scaling and OS patching; the customer is still responsible for code, data and IAM permissions. Scaling the underlying fleet is AWS's job.","Task 2.1",[null,"Lambda scales automatically; AWS manages the underlying servers and capacity.","AWS patches the operating system and managed runtimes beneath Lambda functions.","The physical network is part of security of the cloud and is always AWS's responsibility."],null],["clf27",0,2,"Which of the following is a shared control in the AWS shared responsibility model?",["Physical and environmental controls","Destroying old storage devices","Awareness and training","Classifying customer data"],2,"Awareness and training is shared: AWS trains its employees and customers train theirs. Physical controls and device destruction are AWS-only, while classifying customer data is customer-only.","Task 2.1",["Physical and environmental controls are AWS's alone, because customers have no access to AWS data centers.","Destroying old storage devices is part of AWS's physical media handling; customers never handle AWS hardware.",null,"Classifying customer data is entirely the customer's responsibility, not a shared control."],null],["clf28",0,2,"A new AWS account has just been created. What is the recommended way to handle the root user?",["Create access keys for it to use with the CLI","Share its password with admins through a vault","Enable MFA and use it only for tasks that need it","Delete it once the first IAM user exists"],2,"Protect the root user with MFA, do not create root access keys, and use it only for the few tasks that require it. The root user cannot be deleted, and sharing it removes accountability.","Task 2.3",["Root access keys would give full, long-lived programmatic control of the account; AWS recommends never creating them.","Sharing the root password multiplies the risk; admins should use their own identities with appropriate permissions instead.",null,"The root user cannot be deleted; it exists for the life of the account and must be secured instead."],null],["clf29",0,2,"Which of these tasks can only be performed by the AWS account root user?",["Creating an IAM group for developers","Launching an Amazon EC2 instance","Viewing CloudTrail event history","Closing the AWS account"],3,"Closing the account is one of the tasks that requires root user credentials. An IAM identity with the right permissions can create groups, launch instances and view CloudTrail.","Task 2.3",["Any IAM identity with the right permissions can create IAM groups; root is not required.","Launching EC2 instances only needs IAM permissions, not root credentials.","CloudTrail event history can be viewed by any identity granted CloudTrail read permissions.",null],null],["clf30",0,2,"An application on Amazon EC2 needs to read objects from an S3 bucket. What is the most secure way to give it access?",["Store an IAM user's access keys in the app's config","Attach an IAM role to the instance","Put access keys in the instance's user data","Make the S3 bucket publicly readable"],1,"An IAM role attached through an instance profile provides temporary credentials that rotate automatically. Long-term access keys in files or user data can leak, and a public bucket exposes data to everyone.","Task 2.3",["Storing long-term access keys in a config file risks leaking them and requires manual rotation; a role provides temporary credentials instead.",null,"User data is readable from the instance and is not a secret store, so keys placed there are easy to expose.","A public bucket would let anyone on the internet read the objects, violating least privilege."],null],["clf31",0,2,"What does the principle of least privilege mean?",["Granting only the permissions needed to do a task","Giving every administrator full administrator access","Using the root user for all day-to-day work","Granting permissions to groups only, never roles"],0,"Least privilege means each identity gets only the access it needs, and no more. Broad administrator access for everyone is the opposite.","Task 2.3",[null,"Giving every administrator full access is the opposite of least privilege.","Using the root user daily grants unrestricted access for routine tasks and violates best practice.","Least privilege is about the scope of permissions, not a rule to use groups only; roles are a recommended way to grant limited access."],null],["clf32",0,2,"Fifty developers need the same set of permissions, and new developers join every month. What is the most efficient way to manage this in IAM?",["Attach the policy to each user individually","Share one IAM user sign-in among the team","Grant the team access to the root user","Put the users in an IAM group with the policy"],3,"Attaching the policy to a group means new users get the permissions by joining the group, and changes are made once. Shared sign-ins and root access remove accountability.","Task 2.3",["Attaching the policy to each of fifty users individually is error-prone and hard to maintain as people join and leave.","Sharing one sign-in removes individual accountability, since CloudTrail cannot tell who did what.","The root user has unrestricted access and should never be shared for daily development work.",null],null],["clf33",0,2,"A company with 30 AWS accounts wants its employees to sign in once with their existing corporate identity provider and reach the accounts they are assigned. Which service should it use?",["Amazon Cognito user pools","AWS IAM Identity Center","IAM access keys for every user","AWS Directory Service Simple AD"],1,"IAM Identity Center provides workforce single sign-on across AWS accounts and can federate with an external identity provider. Cognito is designed for customer sign-in to your own applications.","Task 2.3",["Cognito user pools are for customers of web and mobile apps, not workforce access to multiple AWS accounts.",null,"Access keys are long-term credentials for programmatic access and provide no single sign-on across accounts.","Simple AD is a basic managed directory; by itself it does not give single sign-on to assigned AWS accounts through the corporate identity provider."],null],["clf34",0,2,"A mobile app needs sign-up and sign-in for millions of customers, including sign-in with social identity providers. Which AWS service fits?",["Amazon Cognito","AWS IAM Identity Center","AWS Organizations","AWS Directory Service"],0,"Amazon Cognito provides user directories and federation for customers of web and mobile apps. IAM Identity Center is for the workforce accessing AWS accounts and business apps.","Task 2.3",[null,"IAM Identity Center provides workforce sign-in to AWS accounts and business apps, not sign-up for millions of app customers.","AWS Organizations manages multiple AWS accounts and billing; it has no end-user sign-in features.","Directory Service provides managed Microsoft AD style directories for workforce use, not social sign-in for mobile app customers."],null],["clf35",0,2,"An application stores a database password in its source code. The security team wants the password stored centrally and rotated automatically. Which service should they use?",["AWS Certificate Manager","AWS Secrets Manager","AWS KMS","Amazon Macie"],1,"Secrets Manager stores secrets encrypted and can rotate them automatically, for example for RDS databases. KMS manages encryption keys, not application passwords.","Task 2.3",["ACM manages TLS certificates for encryption in transit, not database passwords.",null,"KMS creates and controls encryption keys; it does not store application passwords or rotate database credentials.","Macie discovers sensitive data in S3; it does not store or rotate secrets."],null],["clf36",0,2,"What is the main purpose of AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)?",["Issuing public TLS certificates for websites","Finding sensitive data such as PII in S3","Creating and controlling encryption keys","Storing application passwords with rotation"],2,"KMS creates and manages cryptographic keys and controls their use across AWS services. Public certificates come from AWS Certificate Manager and sensitive-data discovery from Macie.","Task 2.2",["Issuing public TLS certificates is the job of AWS Certificate Manager.","Finding PII in S3 is the job of Amazon Macie.",null,"Storing and rotating application passwords is the job of AWS Secrets Manager, although it uses KMS for encryption."],null],["clf37",0,2,"A company wants free public SSL/TLS certificates for its Application Load Balancer and CloudFront distribution, renewed automatically. Which service provides them?",["AWS CloudHSM","AWS Certificate Manager","AWS Secrets Manager","AWS KMS"],1,"AWS Certificate Manager issues public certificates at no charge for use with integrated services such as ELB and CloudFront, and renews them automatically. CloudHSM provides dedicated hardware for keys, not certificates.","Task 2.2",["CloudHSM provides dedicated hardware security modules for keys; it does not issue or renew TLS certificates.",null,"Secrets Manager stores and rotates secrets such as passwords; it does not issue certificates for load balancers or CloudFront.","KMS manages encryption keys used for data at rest, not public TLS certificates."],null],["clf38",0,2,"A regulator requires a bank to keep its encryption keys in dedicated, single-tenant hardware security modules that the bank alone controls. Which service meets this requirement?",["AWS KMS with AWS managed keys","AWS Secrets Manager","Amazon Macie","AWS CloudHSM"],3,"CloudHSM provides dedicated HSMs in your VPC that you control. AWS managed keys in KMS are managed by AWS in a multi-tenant service, so they do not meet a single-tenant requirement.","Task 2.2",["KMS uses AWS-operated, multi-tenant HSMs, and with AWS managed keys AWS controls the key lifecycle, so it does not meet a single-tenant, customer-only requirement.","Secrets Manager stores secrets encrypted with KMS; it does not provide dedicated HSM hardware.","Macie discovers sensitive data in S3 and has nothing to do with key storage.",null],null],["clf39",0,2,"A security team wants a service that continuously analyzes CloudTrail events, VPC Flow Logs and DNS logs to detect threats such as cryptocurrency mining or unusual API calls. Which service fits?",["Amazon GuardDuty","Amazon Inspector","AWS Shield Standard","Amazon Macie"],0,"GuardDuty is a threat detection service that analyzes these data sources for malicious activity. Inspector looks for software vulnerabilities, not active threats.","Task 2.4",[null,"Inspector scans workloads for software vulnerabilities; it does not analyze CloudTrail, Flow Logs and DNS logs for active threats.","Shield Standard protects against common DDoS attacks; it does not detect crypto mining or unusual API calls.","Macie finds sensitive data in S3; it is not a general threat detection service."],null],["clf40",0,2,"A company must regularly scan its EC2 instances and container images in Amazon ECR for known software vulnerabilities. Which service should it enable?",["Amazon Detective","AWS Config","Amazon Inspector","Amazon GuardDuty"],2,"Amazon Inspector automatically scans EC2, ECR images and Lambda functions for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure. GuardDuty detects threats from log activity rather than scanning software.","Task 2.4",["Detective helps investigate the root cause of existing findings; it does not scan for software vulnerabilities.","AWS Config tracks resource configurations and rule compliance, not CVEs in software packages.",null,"GuardDuty detects malicious activity from logs; it is not a vulnerability scanner for instances and images."],null],["clf41",0,2,"A healthcare company must find out which of its S3 buckets contain personal data such as names and ID numbers. Which service should it use?",["AWS Audit Manager","Amazon Inspector","AWS Artifact","Amazon Macie"],3,"Macie uses machine learning and pattern matching to discover sensitive data in S3. Audit Manager collects evidence for audits; it does not scan bucket contents.","Task 2.4",["Audit Manager collects evidence for audits against frameworks; it does not inspect S3 objects for personal data.","Inspector scans compute workloads for vulnerabilities, not S3 objects for personal data.","Artifact provides AWS compliance reports and agreements; it does not scan your data.",null],null],["clf42",0,2,"A web application behind Amazon CloudFront is being hit by SQL injection attempts. Which service can block these requests with rules?",["AWS Shield Standard","Network ACLs","AWS WAF","Security groups"],2,"AWS WAF inspects HTTP(S) requests and can block common attacks such as SQL injection and cross-site scripting. Shield Standard protects against DDoS at the network and transport layers, and security groups and ACLs filter by IP and port only.","Task 2.4",["Shield Standard mitigates common network and transport layer DDoS attacks; it does not inspect HTTP requests for SQL injection.","Network ACLs filter by IP address, protocol and port at the subnet level and cannot inspect request content.",null,"Security groups allow traffic by port, protocol and source; they cannot recognize SQL injection inside HTTP requests."],null],["clf43",0,2,"Which statement describes AWS Shield Standard?",["A paid service with 24/7 access to the Shield Response Team","A web application firewall configured with HTTP rules","A service that scans hosts for software vulnerabilities","Automatic DDoS protection for all customers at no extra cost"],3,"Shield Standard is on by default for every AWS customer and protects against common network and transport layer DDoS attacks. Access to the Shield Response Team is part of the paid Shield Advanced.","Task 2.4",["This describes Shield Advanced, the paid tier; Shield Standard has no subscription and does not include Shield Response Team access.","This describes AWS WAF, which filters HTTP requests with rules.","This describes Amazon Inspector, a vulnerability management service.",null],null],["clf44",0,2,"An external auditor asks for AWS's SOC 2 report and ISO certifications for the company's compliance file. Where should the company get them?",["AWS Config","AWS Audit Manager","AWS Artifact","AWS Trusted Advisor"],2,"AWS Artifact is the self-service portal for AWS compliance reports and agreements. Audit Manager helps you collect evidence about your own use of AWS, not AWS's third-party reports.","Task 2.2",["AWS Config tracks your own resource configurations; it does not hold AWS's third-party audit reports.","Audit Manager helps collect evidence about your own AWS usage, but AWS's SOC and ISO reports come from AWS Artifact.",null,"Trusted Advisor gives best-practice recommendations for your account, not compliance reports."],null],["clf45",0,2,"An S3 bucket was deleted yesterday, and the team needs to know which identity deleted it and from what IP address. Which service records this?",["AWS CloudTrail","Amazon CloudWatch metrics","VPC Flow Logs","AWS X-Ray"],0,"CloudTrail logs API calls, including who made them, when and from where. CloudWatch metrics are performance data, and VPC Flow Logs record network traffic, not API actions.","Task 2.2",[null,"CloudWatch metrics record performance data such as CPU and request counts, not which identity made an API call.","VPC Flow Logs capture network traffic metadata in a VPC; they do not record S3 API calls or the identity that made them.","X-Ray traces requests through applications for performance debugging; it does not audit account API activity."],null],["clf46",0,2,"A company wants to record configuration changes to its resources and be alerted whenever a security group allows SSH from 0.0.0.0/0. Which service should it use?",["Amazon Inspector","AWS Artifact","Amazon Detective","AWS Config"],3,"AWS Config tracks resource configuration history and evaluates it continuously against rules, such as a rule that flags unrestricted SSH. Inspector scans for software vulnerabilities, and Detective investigates security findings.","Task 2.2",["Inspector scans workloads for software vulnerabilities; it does not record configuration history or evaluate security group rules.","Artifact provides AWS compliance reports and agreements; it does not monitor your resources.","Detective helps investigate findings after the fact; it does not record configuration changes or evaluate rules.",null],null],["clf47",0,2,"Which statement about Amazon VPC security groups is correct?",["They are stateless and evaluated at the subnet","They are stateful and support allow rules only","They support deny rules for chosen IP ranges","They apply to every instance in the Region"],1,"Security groups are stateful (return traffic is allowed automatically), apply to network interfaces and have allow rules only. Stateless, subnet-level rules with explicit denies describe network ACLs.","Task 2.4",["This describes network ACLs, which are stateless and apply at the subnet level.",null,"Security groups cannot contain deny rules; blocking specific IP ranges needs a network ACL.","Security groups apply only to the resources (network interfaces) they are attached to, not to every instance in a Region."],null],["clf48",0,2,"The security team must block one malicious IP range from reaching any instance in a particular subnet. What should they do?",["Add a deny rule to the subnet's network ACL","Add a deny rule to the security group","Detach the internet gateway from the VPC","Enable Shield Standard on the subnet"],0,"Network ACLs support explicit deny rules and apply to the whole subnet. Security groups have no deny rules, and detaching the internet gateway would cut off all internet traffic.","Task 2.4",[null,"Security groups support allow rules only, so there is no way to add a deny rule to one.","Detaching the internet gateway would cut off all internet traffic to the VPC, not just the malicious range.","Shield Standard is already on automatically and protects against DDoS attacks; it is not a per-subnet IP blocking control."],null],["clf49",0,2,"A company uses GuardDuty, Inspector and Macie in several accounts and wants one place to see all findings and check its accounts against security best-practice standards. Which service should it use?",["Amazon Detective","AWS Firewall Manager","AWS Security Hub","AWS Config"],2,"Security Hub aggregates findings from AWS security services and partner tools and runs automated best-practice checks. Detective is for investigating the root cause of a specific finding.","Task 2.4",["Detective investigates the root cause of findings but does not aggregate findings or run best-practice standard checks across accounts.","Firewall Manager centrally applies firewall rules such as WAF and security group policies; it is not a findings dashboard.",null,"AWS Config evaluates resource configurations against rules, but it is not the service that aggregates GuardDuty, Inspector and Macie findings."],null],["clf50",0,3,"Which statement best describes an AWS Availability Zone?",["A group of Regions within a single country","One or more discrete data centers within a Region","A CloudFront site that caches content for users","A single rack of servers in an AWS data center"],1,"Each AZ is one or more data centers with redundant power, networking and connectivity, isolated from other AZs in the same Region. Edge locations are the CloudFront sites.","Task 3.2",["It is the other way round: a Region contains multiple Availability Zones, not a group of Regions.",null,"This describes an edge location, used by CloudFront to cache content.","An AZ is one or more whole data centers with redundant power and networking, not a single rack."],null],["clf51",0,3,"An application must keep running if one AWS data center loses power. What is the recommended approach?",["Use a larger instance type in a single AZ","Keep nightly snapshots in the same AZ","Run instances in two or more AZs behind a load balancer","Move the instances onto a Dedicated Host"],2,"Spreading instances across multiple AZs with a load balancer keeps the application available if one AZ fails. A larger instance or a Dedicated Host is still a single point of failure.","Task 3.2",["A larger instance in one AZ is still a single point of failure if that data center loses power.","Snapshots help recovery but do not keep the application running during an outage, and relying on one AZ leaves it down until restored.",null,"A Dedicated Host is a single physical server, so it adds no protection against a data center power loss."],null],["clf52",0,3,"A company in the European Union must, by law, keep customer data within the EU. What should be its first consideration when choosing an AWS Region?",["The Region with the most Availability Zones","The Region with the lowest On-Demand prices","Compliance and data residency requirements","The Region nearest to AWS headquarters"],2,"Legal and compliance requirements come first because they can rule Regions out entirely; data stays in the Region you choose unless you move it. Price and latency are weighed only among Regions that comply.","Task 3.2",["The number of AZs matters for availability, but a legal residency requirement must be satisfied first.","Price is a valid factor, but it can only be weighed among Regions that already meet the legal requirement.",null,"Distance to AWS headquarters has no bearing on Region choice; data residency law and user proximity do."],null],["clf53",0,3,"A news site wants its images and videos cached close to readers worldwide to reduce latency. Which service should it use?",["Amazon CloudFront","Amazon Route 53","AWS Global Accelerator","AWS Direct Connect"],0,"CloudFront is a content delivery network that caches content at edge locations near users. Global Accelerator improves routing over the AWS network but does not cache content.","Task 3.2",[null,"Route 53 is a DNS service; it directs users to endpoints but does not cache images or videos.","Global Accelerator improves routing over the AWS network using static anycast IPs, but it does not cache content.","Direct Connect is a private connection from a data center to AWS, not a way to deliver content to readers worldwide."],null],["clf54",0,3,"A manufacturer needs to run AWS services on AWS-managed hardware inside its own factory to get very low latency to local systems. Which option fits?",["AWS Outposts","AWS Local Zones","AWS Wavelength","Amazon CloudFront"],0,"Outposts delivers AWS infrastructure and services to your own on-premises location. Local Zones are AWS-operated sites near large cities, not in your building.","Task 3.2",[null,"Local Zones are AWS-operated locations near large cities; they are not installed inside the customer's own factory.","Wavelength embeds AWS infrastructure in telecom providers' 5G networks, not on the customer's premises.","CloudFront caches content at AWS edge locations; it does not run AWS services on hardware in the factory."],null],["clf55",0,3,"A company wants to run code automatically whenever a file is uploaded to an S3 bucket, without provisioning or managing servers. Which service fits best?",["Amazon EC2","Amazon Lightsail","AWS Batch","AWS Lambda"],3,"Lambda runs code in response to events such as S3 uploads and bills only for execution time. EC2 and Lightsail require managing servers that run all the time.","Task 3.3",["EC2 requires you to provision and manage servers, which the company wants to avoid.","Lightsail provides simple virtual private servers that you still manage; it is not event-driven serverless code.","AWS Batch runs batch computing jobs on managed compute environments; it is not the simplest fit for running code on each S3 upload event.",null],null],["clf56",0,3,"A team wants to run containers on Amazon ECS but does not want to provision or patch the underlying EC2 instances. Which option should it choose?",["Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling","AWS Fargate","Amazon Lightsail","AWS Outposts"],1,"Fargate is a serverless compute engine for containers that works with ECS and EKS, so there are no servers to manage. EC2 Auto Scaling still leaves you managing the instances.","Task 3.3",["EC2 Auto Scaling adjusts the number of instances, but you would still provision and patch those instances yourself.",null,"Lightsail is a simplified VPS and container service, not the serverless compute option for Amazon ECS.","Outposts runs AWS infrastructure on premises; you still get hardware to manage capacity on rather than serverless containers."],null],["clf57",0,3,"An order website must keep accepting orders even when the back-end processing service is slow or briefly down, and no order may be lost. Which service should sit between them?",["Amazon SQS","Amazon SNS","AWS Step Functions","Amazon Route 53"],0,"An SQS queue stores messages durably until the processing service can read them, decoupling the two tiers. SNS pushes messages to subscribers immediately and does not hold them for a slow consumer.","Task 3.8",[null,"SNS pushes messages to subscribers immediately and does not hold them in a queue for a slow consumer to pull later, so it is not the buffering choice.","Step Functions orchestrates workflows of steps; it is not a message buffer between a website and a back end.","Route 53 is a DNS service and does not store or deliver orders between application tiers."],null],["clf58",0,3,"A company plans to run a large in-memory database on Amazon EC2. Which instance family category is the best fit?",["Compute optimized","Storage optimized","Accelerated computing","Memory optimized"],3,"Memory optimized instances are designed for workloads that process large data sets in memory. Compute optimized instances favor CPU over memory.","Task 3.3",["Compute optimized instances favor high CPU performance, not the large memory an in-memory database needs.","Storage optimized instances favor high local disk throughput and IOPS rather than large amounts of RAM.","Accelerated computing instances add GPUs or other accelerators for ML and graphics, not extra memory.",null],null],["clf59",0,3,"A developer wants to upload web application code and have AWS handle capacity provisioning, load balancing, scaling and health monitoring, while still being able to see the underlying resources. Which service fits?",["AWS Elastic Beanstalk","AWS CloudFormation","Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling","AWS Systems Manager"],0,"Elastic Beanstalk deploys and manages the environment for your application code. CloudFormation provisions whatever you describe in a template but does not manage the application for you.","Task 3.3",[null,"CloudFormation provisions resources from templates you write; it does not take application code and manage scaling and health for you.","EC2 Auto Scaling adjusts instance counts, but it does not deploy code or set up load balancing and monitoring as a platform.","Systems Manager manages and patches existing instances; it is not a platform for deploying web applications."],null],["clf60",0,3,"A company wants to create identical copies of its environment in several Regions from version-controlled templates. Which service should it use?",["AWS Config","Amazon CloudWatch","AWS Elastic Beanstalk","AWS CloudFormation"],3,"CloudFormation provisions resources repeatably from templates (infrastructure as code). AWS Config records and evaluates configurations but does not create resources.","Task 3.1",["AWS Config records and evaluates resource configurations; it does not create environments from templates.","CloudWatch monitors metrics and logs; it does not provision infrastructure.","Elastic Beanstalk deploys web applications onto managed environments, but it is not the general template-based infrastructure as code service.",null],null],["clf61",0,3,"An administrator wants to automate repetitive AWS tasks with scripts run from a terminal on a laptop. Which access method should they use?",["AWS Management Console","AWS Command Line Interface","AWS Marketplace","AWS re:Post"],1,"The AWS CLI lets you call AWS APIs from a command line and script them. The console is a browser interface meant for interactive use.","Task 3.1",["The console is a graphical, click-based interface and is not suited to scripting repetitive tasks.",null,"AWS Marketplace is a catalog of third-party software, not a way to run commands against AWS.","AWS re:Post is a community Q&A site, not a tool for automating AWS tasks."],null],["clf62",0,3,"A company wants the number of EC2 instances in its web tier to increase when average CPU is high and decrease when it is low. Which service does this?",["Elastic Load Balancing","AWS Compute Optimizer","Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling","Amazon CloudWatch Logs"],2,"EC2 Auto Scaling adds and removes instances based on policies such as target CPU utilization. Elastic Load Balancing spreads traffic across instances but does not change how many there are.","Task 3.3",["Elastic Load Balancing distributes traffic across instances but does not add or remove them.","Compute Optimizer recommends better instance sizes from historical metrics; it does not change instance counts in real time.",null,"CloudWatch Logs stores log data; scaling is driven by metrics and alarms feeding EC2 Auto Scaling, not by Logs itself."],null],["clf63",0,3,"A web application needs HTTP requests to /api routed to one group of instances and /images to another. Which load balancer type should be used?",["Application Load Balancer","Network Load Balancer","Gateway Load Balancer","Route 53 Resolver"],0,"The Application Load Balancer works at layer 7 and supports path-based and host-based routing. The Network Load Balancer works at layer 4 and does not look at URL paths.","Task 3.3",[null,"A Network Load Balancer works at layer 4 (TCP/UDP) and cannot route based on URL paths.","A Gateway Load Balancer is used to deploy virtual appliances such as firewalls, not path-based HTTP routing.","Route 53 Resolver handles DNS resolution in VPCs; it is not a load balancer and cannot route by URL path."],null],["clf64",0,3,"A company wants to move its PostgreSQL database to AWS and stop handling OS patching, backups and hardware failures itself, without changing the database engine. Which option fits?",["Amazon DynamoDB","Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL","Amazon Redshift","PostgreSQL installed on EC2"],1,"Amazon RDS is a managed relational database service that handles patching, backups and failover. Running PostgreSQL on EC2 leaves the OS and database administration with the customer.","Task 3.4",["DynamoDB is a NoSQL database, so moving to it would mean changing the engine and the application's data model.",null,"Redshift is a data warehouse for analytics, not a replacement for a transactional PostgreSQL database.","Running PostgreSQL on EC2 leaves OS patching, backups and recovery from failures with the company."],null],["clf65",0,3,"A gaming company needs a serverless key-value database that delivers single-digit millisecond responses at any scale. Which service should it choose?",["Amazon Aurora","Amazon Neptune","Amazon Redshift","Amazon DynamoDB"],3,"DynamoDB is a fully managed, serverless NoSQL key-value and document database built for consistent low latency at scale. Neptune is a graph database, and Aurora is relational.","Task 3.4",["Aurora is a relational SQL database, not a serverless key-value NoSQL store built for single-digit millisecond access at any scale.","Neptune is a graph database for highly connected data, not a key-value store.","Redshift is a data warehouse for analytical queries, not a low-latency key-value database for a game.",null],null],["clf66",0,3,"Analysts need to run complex SQL queries across several petabytes of historical sales data for reporting. Which service is designed for this?",["Amazon Redshift","Amazon RDS","Amazon ElastiCache","Amazon DocumentDB"],0,"Redshift is a data warehouse built for analytic queries on very large data sets. RDS is optimized for transactional workloads, not petabyte-scale analytics.","Task 3.4",[null,"RDS runs transactional relational databases and is not designed for analytics across petabytes.","ElastiCache is an in-memory cache, not a store for petabytes of historical data.","DocumentDB is a document database for JSON-style application data, not a SQL data warehouse."],null],["clf67",0,3,"A popular website repeatedly reads the same product data from its database, which is becoming overloaded. Which service can cache these results in memory?",["Amazon S3 Glacier","Amazon EBS","Amazon ElastiCache","AWS Storage Gateway"],2,"ElastiCache provides managed in-memory caches (such as Redis OSS, Valkey and Memcached) to reduce database load and latency. EBS is block storage for instances, not a cache.","Task 3.4",["S3 Glacier is archival storage with slow retrieval, the opposite of a fast in-memory cache.","EBS is block storage attached to one instance; it does not provide an in-memory cache in front of a database.",null,"Storage Gateway connects on-premises applications to cloud storage; it is not a database cache."],null],["clf68",0,3,"What is the main purpose of an Amazon RDS Multi-AZ deployment?",["Serving read traffic from replicas in other Regions","Reducing storage costs by compressing data","High availability through automatic failover","Letting the database run outside of a VPC"],2,"Multi-AZ keeps a standby in another AZ and fails over automatically if the primary fails. Offloading reads is the job of read replicas.","Task 3.4",["Read replicas, not Multi-AZ standbys, are used to serve read traffic, and cross-Region replicas are a separate feature.","Multi-AZ adds a standby copy and actually increases storage cost; it does not compress data.",null,"RDS databases run inside a VPC; Multi-AZ does not change that."],null],["clf69",0,3,"EC2 instances in a private subnet must download software updates from the internet, but must not accept connections started from the internet. What should be used?",["An internet gateway route in the private subnet","A NAT gateway in a public subnet","A VPC peering connection","A virtual private gateway"],1,"A NAT gateway lets private instances start outbound connections while blocking unsolicited inbound connections. Routing the private subnet straight to an internet gateway would make it a public subnet.","Task 3.5",["A route to an internet gateway would make the subnet public and allow inbound connections to instances with public IPs.",null,"VPC peering connects two VPCs privately; it does not provide internet access.","A virtual private gateway terminates VPN or Direct Connect connections to on-premises networks, not general internet access."],null],["clf70",0,3,"A company needs a dedicated private network connection from its data center to AWS with consistent bandwidth, rather than a connection over the public internet. What should it use?",["AWS Site-to-Site VPN","Amazon CloudFront","AWS Direct Connect","VPC peering"],2,"Direct Connect provides a dedicated private link to AWS with more consistent performance. Site-to-Site VPN is encrypted but runs over the internet, so performance varies.","Task 3.5",["Site-to-Site VPN runs encrypted tunnels over the public internet, so bandwidth can vary with internet conditions.","CloudFront delivers content to end users from edge locations; it does not connect a data center to AWS.",null,"VPC peering connects two VPCs to each other, not an on-premises data center to AWS."],null],["clf71",0,3,"Which set of capabilities does Amazon Route 53 provide?",["Content caching at edge locations","Private connectivity to on-premises sites","Load balancing inside a single AZ only","DNS, domain registration and health checks"],3,"Route 53 is AWS's DNS service, with domain registration, routing policies and health checks. Caching at edge locations is CloudFront.","Task 3.5",["Caching content at edge locations is what Amazon CloudFront does.","Private connectivity to on-premises sites comes from Site-to-Site VPN or Direct Connect.","Load balancing within an AZ is not Route 53's role; it provides DNS-based routing policies across endpoints, while Elastic Load Balancing spreads traffic across AZs.",null],null],["clf72",0,3,"A company needs highly durable storage for millions of product images accessed over HTTPS by its web app. Which service is the best fit?",["Amazon EBS","Amazon S3","Amazon EFS","EC2 instance store"],1,"S3 is object storage designed for 99.999999999% durability and web-scale access. Instance store is temporary and loses data when the instance stops.","Task 3.6",["EBS volumes attach to individual instances and are not accessed over HTTPS by a web app at scale.",null,"EFS is a shared file system mounted over NFS, not object storage accessed over HTTPS.","Instance store is temporary and loses data when the instance stops, so it is not durable."],null],["clf73",0,3,"Legal records must be kept for 10 years, are almost never read, and a retrieval time of up to 12 hours is acceptable. Which S3 storage class has the lowest storage cost?",["S3 Standard-Infrequent Access","S3 Glacier Deep Archive","S3 Intelligent-Tiering","S3 Standard"],1,"Glacier Deep Archive is the lowest-cost storage class, meant for long-term retention with retrievals measured in hours. Standard-IA is for data that needs fast access when it is read.","Task 3.6",["Standard-IA costs more to store than the Glacier classes; it is meant for data needing fast access when read.",null,"Intelligent-Tiering adds monitoring charges and suits unknown access patterns; for known long-term archives Deep Archive is cheaper.","S3 Standard is the most expensive class to store and is meant for frequently accessed data."],null],["clf74",0,3,"A company stores data with changing and unpredictable access patterns and wants S3 to move objects between access tiers automatically to save money. Which storage class should it use?",["S3 One Zone-IA","S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval","S3 Standard","S3 Intelligent-Tiering"],3,"Intelligent-Tiering monitors access and moves objects between tiers automatically without retrieval fees. One Zone-IA is cheaper storage in one AZ but does not move data for you.","Task 3.6",["One Zone-IA is a fixed class for infrequently accessed, re-creatable data; it does not move objects between tiers automatically.","Glacier Flexible Retrieval is an archive class with retrieval delays; it does not tier data automatically based on access.","S3 Standard keeps all data in one tier regardless of how often it is accessed.",null],null],["clf75",0,3,"An EC2 instance needs a boot volume whose data persists when the instance is stopped and started. Which storage should it use?",["Amazon EBS","Amazon S3","Amazon EFS","Instance store"],0,"EBS volumes are persistent block storage attached to an instance in one AZ. Instance store data is lost when the instance stops.","Task 3.6",[null,"S3 is object storage accessed over the network; an EC2 instance cannot use it as a block boot volume.","EFS is a shared network file system, not a boot volume for an instance.","Instance store data is lost when the instance is stopped, so it does not persist."],null],["clf76",0,3,"Dozens of Linux instances across three Availability Zones need to read and write the same files at the same time through a standard file system. Which service fits?",["Amazon EBS","Amazon FSx for Windows File Server","Amazon EFS","Amazon S3 Glacier"],2,"EFS is a managed NFS file system that many Linux instances can mount across AZs. FSx for Windows File Server uses SMB and is aimed at Windows workloads.","Task 3.6",["A standard EBS volume attaches to one instance in a single AZ, so it cannot serve dozens of instances across three AZs.","FSx for Windows File Server provides SMB shares for Windows workloads; for Linux instances using a standard NFS file system, EFS is the natural fit.",null,"S3 Glacier is archival object storage, not a file system that instances can read and write concurrently."],null],["clf77",0,3,"On-premises applications must keep using local file shares while the data itself is stored in Amazon S3 with a local cache. Which service provides this hybrid setup?",["AWS DataSync","Amazon EFS","AWS Backup","AWS Storage Gateway"],3,"Storage Gateway's S3 File Gateway presents NFS or SMB shares on premises and stores files as S3 objects. DataSync moves data between locations but does not present an ongoing file share.","Task 3.6",["DataSync transfers data between locations but does not present ongoing local file shares with a cache.","EFS is a cloud file system for AWS compute; on-premises applications would not get a local cache backed by S3.","AWS Backup manages backups; it does not provide file shares for on-premises applications.",null],null],["clf78",0,3,"An insurance company wants to extract printed text, forms and tables from scanned claim documents automatically. Which service should it use?",["Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Textract","Amazon Rekognition","Amazon Polly"],1,"Textract extracts text, handwriting, forms and tables from documents. Comprehend analyzes text that is already extracted (for sentiment or entities), and Rekognition analyzes images and video.","Task 3.7",["Comprehend analyzes the meaning of text you already have, such as sentiment and entities; it does not read scanned documents.",null,"Rekognition analyzes images and video for objects, faces and scenes; it is not built to extract forms and tables from documents.","Polly converts text to speech, the opposite of extracting text from documents."],null],["clf79",0,3,"A company wants to build a generative AI chatbot using foundation models through an API, without training models or managing infrastructure. Which service fits best?",["Amazon SageMaker AI","Amazon Bedrock","Amazon Kendra","Amazon Transcribe"],1,"Bedrock is a fully managed service offering foundation models through an API. SageMaker AI is aimed at building, training and deploying your own ML models, which is more work than needed here.","Task 3.7",["SageMaker AI is for building, training and deploying your own models, which is more work than the team wants.",null,"Kendra is an intelligent enterprise search service; it does not provide access to foundation models for a chatbot.","Transcribe converts speech to text; it does not generate chatbot responses."],null],["clf80",0,3,"An analyst wants to run SQL queries directly on CSV log files stored in Amazon S3, paying per query and without loading the data into a database. Which service should they use?",["Amazon Athena","Amazon Redshift","Amazon RDS","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams"],0,"Athena is a serverless query service that runs SQL against data in S3 and charges for the data scanned. Redshift would require provisioning a warehouse and loading or mapping the data.","Task 3.7",[null,"Redshift is a data warehouse that normally requires loading data (Spectrum aside) and provisioned or serverless capacity; it is not the simple per-query tool for S3 files.","RDS requires loading data into a database instance, which the analyst wants to avoid.","Kinesis Data Streams ingests real-time streaming data; it does not run SQL queries on files in S3."],null],["clf81",0,4,"A database server will run 24 hours a day for the next three years with steady usage. Which pricing option will usually cost the least?",["On-Demand Instances","Spot Instances","A 3-year Savings Plan","On-Demand Dedicated Hosts"],2,"Savings Plans (and Reserved Instances) give large discounts in exchange for a 1- or 3-year commitment on steady usage. Spot is cheaper per hour but can be interrupted, which a database server cannot tolerate.","Task 4.1",["On-Demand is the most expensive option for steady, long-running usage because there is no commitment discount.","Spot capacity can be interrupted with a two-minute warning, which a 24/7 database cannot tolerate.",null,"Dedicated Hosts at On-Demand rates are the most expensive option and are only justified by licensing or compliance needs."],null],["clf82",0,4,"A company runs nightly image-processing batch jobs that can stop and resume without problems. Which EC2 purchase option is the most cost-effective?",["Dedicated Instances","On-Demand Instances","Reserved Instances","Spot Instances"],3,"Spot Instances use spare capacity at deep discounts and suit interruptible, flexible workloads. Reserved Instances need a 1- or 3-year commitment that a nightly batch job may not use fully.","Task 4.1",["Dedicated Instances cost more than standard instances and give no benefit for interruptible batch jobs.","On-Demand works but costs much more than Spot for jobs that can tolerate interruption.","Reserved Instances need a 1- or 3-year commitment and suit steady usage, not nightly jobs that could run on cheaper spare capacity.",null],null],["clf83",0,4,"Before building anything, a team wants to estimate the monthly cost of a planned architecture with EC2, RDS and S3. Which tool should it use?",["AWS Cost Explorer","AWS Pricing Calculator","AWS Budgets","AWS Cost and Usage Report"],1,"AWS Pricing Calculator estimates costs for services you have not deployed yet. Cost Explorer analyzes spending that has already happened.","Task 4.2",["Cost Explorer analyzes costs you have already incurred, so it cannot estimate an architecture that does not exist yet.",null,"AWS Budgets tracks actual or forecast spending against thresholds; it does not price a planned design.","The Cost and Usage Report contains detailed data on past usage, not estimates for planned resources."],null],["clf84",0,4,"A manager wants an email when the account's monthly spend is forecast to go over $500. Which service should be configured?",["AWS Cost Explorer","AWS Pricing Calculator","AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS Budgets"],3,"AWS Budgets sends alerts when actual or forecasted cost or usage crosses a threshold. Cost Explorer shows forecasts but is not where you set threshold alerts.","Task 4.2",["Cost Explorer shows spending and forecasts in charts, but it does not send threshold alerts by email.","The Pricing Calculator estimates planned costs; it does not watch actual spending.","Trusted Advisor gives best-practice recommendations; it does not alert on forecast spend thresholds.",null],null],["clf85",0,4,"A finance analyst wants to view the last six months of spending by service in charts and see a forecast for the coming months. Which tool should they use?",["AWS Cost Explorer","AWS Pricing Calculator","AWS Artifact","AWS Budgets"],0,"Cost Explorer visualizes historical cost and usage and provides forecasts. The Pricing Calculator only estimates planned architectures.","Task 4.2",[null,"The Pricing Calculator estimates planned architectures; it does not show your past spending.","Artifact provides compliance reports, not cost data.","Budgets alerts on thresholds, but the charts of past spending by service with forecasts are Cost Explorer's job."],null],["clf86",0,4,"A company has 12 AWS accounts and wants a single bill and combined usage so it reaches volume pricing tiers sooner. What should it use?",["Cost allocation tags on each resource","A separate Savings Plan in each account","Consolidated billing in AWS Organizations","AWS Budgets set in every account"],2,"Consolidated billing combines usage across member accounts for volume discounts and produces one bill. Cost allocation tags categorize spending but do not combine accounts.","Task 4.2",["Cost allocation tags help attribute costs but do not combine bills or usage across accounts.","Separate Savings Plans do not combine usage into one bill or pool volume tiers across accounts.",null,"Budgets alert on spending; they do not merge bills or aggregate usage for volume pricing."],null],["clf87",0,4,"Several projects share one AWS account, and finance needs each project's share of the monthly bill. What should the company use?",["Separate IAM groups for each project","Cost allocation tags such as Project","AWS Pricing Calculator estimates","Descriptive security group names"],1,"Activated cost allocation tags appear in billing reports and Cost Explorer so costs can be grouped by project. IAM groups control permissions and have no effect on cost reporting.","Task 4.2",["IAM groups control permissions; they do not break down costs on the bill.",null,"Pricing Calculator estimates are forecasts, not a record of each project's actual share of the bill.","Security group names do not appear as cost dimensions in billing reports; activated cost allocation tags do."],null],["clf88",0,4,"A large company wants a designated Technical Account Manager who knows its environment and provides proactive guidance. Which AWS Support plan includes this?",["Basic Support","Developer Support","Business Support","Enterprise Support"],3,"A designated TAM is part of Enterprise Support. Basic Support includes account and billing help, documentation and core Trusted Advisor checks, but no technical case support or TAM.","Task 4.3",["Basic Support is free and includes no technical support cases, let alone a TAM.","Developer Support provides business-hours email access to support engineers but no TAM.","Business Support adds 24/7 technical support and full Trusted Advisor checks, but not a designated TAM.",null],null],["clf89",0,4,"A company notices that an IP address belonging to AWS is sending spam and attacking its servers. Whom should it contact to report the abuse?",["AWS Professional Services","The AWS Trust & Safety team","The AWS Partner Network","AWS Marketplace sellers"],1,"The AWS Trust & Safety team handles reports of abuse coming from AWS resources. Professional Services and partners help with customer projects, not abuse reports.","Task 4.3",["AWS Professional Services helps customers deliver cloud projects; it does not handle abuse reports.",null,"The AWS Partner Network is a community of partner companies, not the channel for reporting abuse from AWS resources.","Marketplace sellers offer third-party products and have no role in handling abuse from AWS IP addresses."],null],["clf90",0,4,"Which type of data transfer is generally free of charge on AWS?",["Data transferred into AWS from the internet","Data transferred out of AWS to the internet","Data transferred between two AWS Regions","Data processed by a NAT gateway"],0,"Inbound data transfer from the internet is generally free. Outbound transfer to the internet and transfer between Regions are charged, and NAT gateways charge for data processed.","Task 4.1",[null,"Data transferred out of AWS to the internet is charged per gigabyte.","Data transferred between AWS Regions incurs data transfer charges.","NAT gateways charge per hour and per gigabyte of data processed."],null]]);
